WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.249
Serialization contracts often live longer than a single release.

2
00:00:04.249 --> 00:00:07.038
API clients may update at different times.

3
00:00:07.038 --> 00:00:09.760
Queue messages may be delayed or retried.

4
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.947
Stored serialized data may be read months later.

5
00:00:12.947 --> 00:00:16.068
Export files may be imported by external tools.

6
00:00:16.068 --> 00:00:21.114
Event messages may be consumed by teams that deploy on a different schedule.

7
00:00:21.114 --> 00:00:24.500
Secure design assumes old and new data may coexist.

8
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:32.136
Versioning should cover API payloads, queue messages, event messages, imports, exports, and stored serialized data.

9
00:00:32.136 --> 00:00:44.817
A version does not need to be complicated, but producers and consumers should know which fields are required, which are optional, which are deprecated, and how unknown data should be handled.

10
00:00:44.817 --> 00:00:49.731
Ownership should be clear so teams know who can approve a contract change.

11
00:00:49.731 --> 00:00:53.316
Backward and forward compatibility require discipline.

12
00:00:53.316 --> 00:00:59.756
Adding an optional field may be safe if old consumers ignore it and new consumers handle absence.

13
00:00:59.756 --> 00:01:06.462
Renaming or removing a field can be disruptive if stored data or external clients still depend on it.

14
00:01:06.462 --> 00:01:11.907
Changing defaults can alter behavior even when the serialized shape looks similar.

15
00:01:11.907 --> 00:01:14.297
Compatibility needs tests, not hope.

16
00:01:14.297 --> 00:01:19.410
Avoid unsafe shortcuts when older data no longer matches newer object models.

17
00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:27.510
Broad acceptance, loose conversions, or hidden fallback behavior can mask a migration problem and produce ambiguous state.

18
00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:39.196
A safer approach is to identify affected data, define migration rules, preserve enough context for diagnostics, and reject or quarantine data that cannot be interpreted safely.

19
00:01:39.196 --> 00:01:45.835
Migration strategies for stored serialized data should be planned at a high level before deployment.

20
00:01:45.835 --> 00:01:54.998
Teams may migrate eagerly, migrate on read, maintain a compatibility reader for a limited period, or support multiple versions explicitly.

21
00:01:54.998 --> 00:01:57.654
Each approach has operational tradeoffs.

22
00:01:57.654 --> 00:02:02.567
What matters is that the path is documented, tested, monitored, and owned.

23
00:02:02.567 --> 00:02:05.090
Contract tests help prevent surprises.

24
00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:16.776
Before deployment, test old producer to new consumer, new producer to old consumer where required, missing fields, unknown fields, deprecated fields, and incompatible versions.

25
00:02:16.776 --> 00:02:24.146
The goal is to make compatibility behavior predictable and visible before real production data exposes the gap.
